{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies small-scale possession as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or meth can be offered a path to services instead of prosecution—if they meet strict conditions. Those who don’t qualify may face up to six months behind bars. Law enforcement now has the authority to act more decisively in public spaces, especially near areas like playgrounds, and penalties for distribution have been strengthened.\n\n***\n\nThe revised law invites—but doesn’t require—local jurisdictions to set up alternative pathways focused on health and support. While proponents argue this flexibility lets regions tailor responses to local needs, critics warn it could lead to uneven outcomes. Police are instructed to connect eligible individuals with mobile health teams within half an hour; if the team doesn’t arrive in time, arrest becomes the default. Eligibility hinges on having no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and being medically stable—conditions that many argue are too narrow to be practical.\n\n***\n\nPortland’s police chief emphasized the time-sensitive nature of the process. Officers can’t linger; if the health response doesn’t show up quickly, the individual goes to jail. This system places pressure on both law enforcement and health services to coordinate seamlessly, even as resources remain stretched. Some fear that without guaranteed funding or standardized protocols, the new model may fail those most in need.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national reckoning with how society addresses dependency. In a country where access to care remains uneven, even simple acts like eating a meal or shopping for groceries can feel out of reach for those struggling. The tongue of policy, sharp and unyielding, often fails to taste the real hunger beneath.  \n\nP.P.S The age of accountability is here, and in this country, even the simplest acts—like eating, shopping, or speaking with a clear tongue—can be shaped by laws meant to heal but often only punish.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.1979886293411255}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.020046139135956764}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.17481629550457}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.05539166182279587}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.6464111215318553e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.018910711631178856}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.26316261291503906}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.24863916635513306}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00019608005823101848}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.5668477416038513}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.013901433907449245}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.9236127138137817}, {"token": "-scale", "logprob": -0.17570240795612335}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.00018082413589581847}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.3484598994255066}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00017152745567727834}, {"token": " 